
#
# \brief  Criterion 18 third-party settings acceptance (W4):
#        settings_demo's two provider-owned entries — discovery, first
#        paint, keystroke validation, package-private persistence,
#        close/restart retention, removal/reinstall, never-aliased
#        stores, and no shared-DE configd route or capability.
# \author Sponge OS contributors
# \date   2026
#
# WHAT THIS PROVES (fail-loud; every gate is bounded and named):
#
#   1. TWO INDEPENDENT ENTRIES: after `vct install`-equivalent install
#      over the pkgd request channel, the `installed` broadcast carries
#      exactly two <settings> entries (docs/12-package-format.md
#      §14.4) with the exact §14.2 field sets — (settings_demo,
#      greeting) and (settings_demo, about) — distinct targets
#      (settings_demo_greeting / settings_demo_about) and distinct
#      icons. Each open answers its own '<pkg>.<id>' start identity:
#      sponge_pkgd: settings result settings_demo 'greeting' -> ok started
#      sponge_pkgd: settings result settings_demo 'about'    -> ok started
#   2. FIRST PAINT per provider: the probe captures the composited
#      screen and requires the provider header band (#27273a, painted
#      by settings_demo/common/paint.h) inside the window content
#      rect reported by the layouter's window_layout — a pixel
#      witness, not a log claim. The construction witness is each
#      provider's 'package-private store enabled at ...' line.
#   3. VALIDATION + PERSISTENCE: probe-injected keystrokes drive the
#      REAL input chain (nitpicker Event -> wm -> provider Gui input):
#      '@' is REJECTED with the provider's exact English error
#      ('Greeting has an invalid character (allowed: ...)') and never
#      becomes the value; "Hi there" + Enter persists; the probe then
#      reads the package-private store files byte-for-byte:
#        /greeting.xml = <settings-demo version="1"><entry
#                        name="greeting" value="Hi there"/></settings-demo>
#        /about.xml    = <settings-demo version="1"><entry
#                        name="note" value="Note A"/></settings-demo>
#      Distinct entry names and distinct values: the stores are
#      per-provider and never alias (D16.10 tier (b), §14.6).
#   4. CLOSE/RESTART RETENTION: a dirty append (no Enter) followed by
#      the PRODUCTION close path — hover-gated decorator-closer click
#      -> window_layouter close -> wm resize_request 0x0 -> the
#      provider's Gui-info 'closed' channel -> close-COMMIT + exit
#      (docs/05 §4.6). Evidence chain per provider:
#        settings_demo_greeting: persisted greeting   (commit AT close)
#        settings_demo_greeting: window closed, exiting
#        sponge_pkgd: settings provider settings_demo.greeting exited
#                     (0) -> claim released; next open starts fresh
#        settings_demo_greeting: restored greeting from store
#      and the store carries the dirty value afterwards ("Hi there!",
#      "Note A ok"). State is retained across a provider close/restart.
#   5. REMOVAL/REINSTALL: `remove settings_demo` deletes the entries
#      from discovery at once and prunes both claims; later opens of
#      either entry answer not-installed (§14.7 stale-open). A
#      reinstall plus later opens answer started as appropriate, and
#      each provider restores ITS OWN store value — the two stores
#      stay un-aliased across the whole remove/install cycle.
#   6. NO SHARED-DE CONFIGD: the staged metadata carries only the Gui
#      and File_system <sessions> (audited below before boot), no
#      configd component exists in this topology, and the post-gate
#      regexp requires ZERO 'configd' occurrences in the whole boot.
#      The package-private File_system path is proved writable by the
#      provider persists AND by the probe's own sentinel
#      write/read-back/unlink on the same <fs/> route.
#
# DRIVER: settings_thirdparty_probe (repos/sponge/src/test/
# settings_thirdparty_probe/) drives everything itself — install/
# remove over the pkgd request channel, settings opens over the
# settings_request channel, input via nitpicker's Event service
# (the sponge_de_probe inject path), pixel witnesses via Capture,
# and exact store XML via its own File_system session on the
# package-private vfs_store route. Every probe step logs a
# 'settings-thirdparty-probe: ...' marker; the run script gates each
# one (bounded) and interleaves the provider/pkgd log gates, then
# post-gates exact line counts and prints the explicit PASS verdict.
#
# TOPOLOGY (the sponge-settings-center.run report_rom/pkgd/
# pkg_runtime/vfs_store skeleton with the sponge-wm.run wm trio
# substituted for bare nitpicker hosting, because the close-to-commit
# contract needs the v1.1 app-visible close channel — only wm writes
# the Gui-info 'capture closed' flag):
#
#   settings_thirdparty_probe -> request          -> sponge_pkgd `request`
#   settings_thirdparty_probe -> settings_request -> sponge_pkgd `settings_request`  [<settings_request/>]
#   sponge_pkgd `result` / `settings_result` / `installed` -> probe
#   sponge_pkgd `runtime`          -> pkg_runtime `config`
#   pkg_runtime `state`            -> sponge_pkgd `runtime_state`   [<settings_liveness/>]
#   wm/layouter/decorator report matrix (window_list, window_layout,
#     hover, focus, resize_request, rules, decorator_margins, pointer)
#   providers (settings_demo.greeting / settings_demo.about inside
#     pkg_runtime) -> Gui -> wm -> nitpicker; File_system -> vfs_store
#     (the metadata subpath "/app/settings_demo" materialized as the
#     session label; policy root / writeable yes)
#
# There is deliberately NO sponge_configd, NO sponge-de, NO shared-DE
# route of any kind in this topology (§14.6: third-party providers
# cannot write shared DE keys at all).
#
# base-sel4 only: the acceptance target is the production kernel on
# QEMU (the center scenario's assertion, kept).
#

assert {[have_spec sel4]} \
	"sponge-settings-thirdparty requires KERNEL=sel4 (BOARD=pc) in build.conf."

build { core lib/ld init timer server/report_rom server/nitpicker \
        server/wm app/window_layouter app/decorator \
        server/vfs lib/vfs \
        sponge_pkgd settings_demo/greeting settings_demo/about \
        test/settings_thirdparty_probe }

create_boot_directory

install_config {
config
+ parent-provides
  + service ROM
  + service PD
  + service RM
  + service CPU
  + service LOG
  + service IRQ
  + service IO_MEM
  + service IO_PORT

+ default | caps: 200 | ram: 4M
+ default-route
  + any-service
    + parent
    + any-child

+ start timer
  + provides | + service Timer

#
# report_rom - the single relay. Full label chains, one writer per
# label (AGENTS.md 1.2). The wm trio matrix is the sponge-wm.run
# matrix with the probe fan-outs added; the pkgd matrix is the
# sponge-settings-center.run matrix with the probe as the request
# writer. No policy names any configd label.
#
+ start report_rom | caps: 400 | ram: 8M
  + provides
    + service Report
    + service ROM
  + config | verbose: no
    + policy | label: layouter -> window_list       | report: wm -> window_list
    + policy | label: decorator -> pointer          | report: wm -> pointer
    + policy | label: decorator -> window_layout    | report: layouter -> window_layout
    + policy | label: wm -> focus                   | report: layouter -> focus
    + policy | label: wm -> resize_request          | report: layouter -> resize_request
    + policy | label: layouter -> hover             | report: decorator -> hover
    + policy | label: layouter -> decorator_margins | report: decorator -> decorator_margins
    + policy | label: layouter -> rules             | report: layout_rules
    + policy | label: layouter -> focus_request     | report: wm -> focus_request
    + policy | label: pointer -> hover              | report: nitpicker -> hover
    + policy | label: pointer -> xray               | report: nitpicker -> xray
    + policy | label: settings_thirdparty_probe -> window_list     | report: wm -> window_list
    + policy | label: settings_thirdparty_probe -> window_layout   | report: layouter -> window_layout
    + policy | label: settings_thirdparty_probe -> hover           | report: decorator -> hover
    + policy | label: settings_thirdparty_probe -> focus           | report: layouter -> focus
    + policy | label: sponge_pkgd -> request          | report: settings_thirdparty_probe -> request
    + policy | label: sponge_pkgd -> settings_request | report: settings_thirdparty_probe -> settings_request
    + policy | label: sponge_pkgd -> runtime_state    | report: pkg_runtime -> state
    + policy | label: settings_thirdparty_probe -> result          | report: sponge_pkgd -> result
    + policy | label: settings_thirdparty_probe -> settings_result | report: sponge_pkgd -> settings_result
    + policy | label: settings_thirdparty_probe -> installed       | report: sponge_pkgd -> installed
    + policy | label: pkg_runtime -> config           | report: sponge_pkgd -> runtime

#
# nitpicker - headless compositing (Capture defines the panorama; no
# framebuffer driver is needed for these claims). One domain: the wm
# owns every window on screen.
#
+ start nitpicker | caps: 400 | ram: 32M
  + provides
    + service Gui
    + service Capture
    + service Event
  + route
    + service Report | + child report_rom
    + service Timer | + child timer
    + any-service
      + parent
      + any-child
  + config
    + capture
    + event
    + report | focus: yes | hover: yes | xray: yes
    + domain apps | layer: 1 | content: client | label: no
                  | hover: always | focus: click
    + default-policy | domain: apps
    + background | color: #1e1e2e

#
# wm - upstream window manager (the only writer of the app-visible
# close channel the providers commit on, docs/05 4.6).
#
+ start wm | caps: 250 | ram: 8M
  + provides
    + service Gui
    + service Report
    + service ROM
  + config
    + policy | label_prefix: decorator | role: decorator
    + policy | label_prefix: layouter  | role: layouter
    + default-policy
  + route
    + service ROM | label: focus          | + child report_rom
    + service ROM | label: resize_request | + child report_rom
    + service Report | label_last: shape  | + child report_rom
    + service Report | + child report_rom
    + service Gui | + child nitpicker
    + any-service
      + parent
      + any-child

#
# layouter - window_layouter with static per-entry assigns, so the
# two provider windows never overlap and their content rects are
# known exactly (the probe clicks and pixel-samples by position).
# The assign label prefixes are the wm window_list labels of the two
# generated settings start identities.
#
+ start layouter | caps: 150 | ram: 4M
  + binary window_layouter
  + config | rules: rom
    + report | rules: yes
    + rules
      + screen screen
      + assign | label_prefix: pkg_runtime -> settings_demo.greeting | target: screen
               | xpos: 40 | ypos: 40 | width: 440 | height: 240 | maximized: no
      + assign | label_prefix: pkg_runtime -> settings_demo.about | target: screen
               | xpos: 40 | ypos: 380 | width: 440 | height: 240 | maximized: no
    + press   | key: BTN_LEFT  | action: drag
      + release | key: BTN_LEFT | action: drop
  + route
    + service ROM | label: window_list       | + child report_rom
    + service ROM | label: focus_request     | + child report_rom
    + service ROM | label: hover             | + child report_rom
    + service ROM | label: decorator_margins | + child report_rom
    + service ROM | label: rules             | + child report_rom
    + service Report | label: rules          | + child report_rom | label: layout_rules
    + service Report | + child report_rom
    + any-service
      + child wm
      + parent
      + any-child

#
# decorator - the stock motif decorator with the title bar and the
# closer control (the providers' own window chrome, docs/05 4.6).
#
+ start decorator | caps: 350 | ram: 32M
  + config
    + controls
      + title
      + closer
  + route
    + service ROM | label: window_layout | + child report_rom
    + service ROM | label: pointer       | + child report_rom
    + service Report | + child report_rom
    + any-service
      + child wm
      + parent
      + any-child

#
# vfs_store - the provider package-private store backend (docs/12 13
# and 14.6). Ram-backed; the persistence-restart claim is the
# provider close/restart leg above, and the File_system path is
# proved writable by the provider persists plus the probe sentinel.
#
+ start vfs_store | caps: 150 | ram: 16M
  + binary vfs
  + provides | + service File_system
  + config
    + vfs
      + dir app
        + dir settings_demo
          + ram
    + policy | label_prefix: /app/settings_demo | root: / | writeable: yes
  + route
    + service ROM | label_last: ld.lib.so | + parent
    + service ROM | label_last: vfs.lib.so | + parent
    + any-service
      + parent
      + any-child

#
# sponge_pkgd - the settings metadata/open transport. The two opt-in
# gates below are the component-config opt-ins the wiring contract
# requires: settings_request arms the open channel and
# settings_liveness arms claim release on provider exit (watching
# pkg_runtime state via runtime_state). No vfs node: no pkgd store.
#
+ start sponge_pkgd | caps: 300 | ram: 32M
  + config
    + settings_request
    + settings_liveness
  + route
    + service ROM | label_last: request          | + child report_rom
    + service ROM | label_last: settings_request | + child report_rom
    + service ROM | label_last: runtime_state    | + child report_rom
    + service Report | + child report_rom
    + any-service
      + parent
      + any-child

#
# pkg_runtime - nested init hosting the launched providers. Its
# config is the pkgd runtime report. Gui resolves to wm (decorated
# windows with the production closer); File_system resolves to the
# package-private vfs_store with the metadata subpath materialized
# as the session label.
#
+ start pkg_runtime | caps: 2000 | ram: 256M
  + binary init
  + route
    + service ROM | label: config | + child report_rom
    + service Gui | + child wm
    + service File_system | + child vfs_store | label: /app/settings_demo/
    + service Report | + child report_rom
    + any-service
      + parent
      + any-child

#
# settings_thirdparty_probe - the whole scenario driver (see the
# header comment). Capability surface: Capture + Event + Timer, the
# pkgd request/result report channels, the wm window_list/
# window_layout/hover/focus ROMs, and one File_system session on the
# package-private store route. No Gui, no configd, no ROM beyond
# those.
#
+ start settings_thirdparty_probe | caps: 500 | ram: 32M
  + config
    + vfs
      + fs
  + route
    + service Timer   | + child timer
    + service Capture | + child nitpicker
    + service Event   | + child nitpicker
    + service File_system | + child vfs_store | label: /app/settings_demo/
    + service ROM | label_last: result          | + child report_rom
    + service ROM | label_last: settings_result | + child report_rom
    + service ROM | label_last: installed       | + child report_rom
    + service ROM | label_last: window_list     | + child report_rom
    + service ROM | label_last: window_layout   | + child report_rom
    + service ROM | label_last: hover           | + child report_rom
    + service ROM | label_last: focus           | + child report_rom
    + service Report | + child report_rom
    + any-service
      + parent
      + any-child
- }

#
# Stage the settings_demo package metadata, the derived index and the
# two entry icons (docs/12 14.2: icons are ordinary staged payload
# images) as boot modules.
#
set staged_pkgs { settings_demo }
foreach pkg $staged_pkgs {
	set src "${genode_dir}/../pkg/$pkg/metadata.xml"
	if {![file exists $src]} {
		error "package metadata not found: $src"
	}
	exec cp -L $src "bin/pkg_$pkg.xml"
}

set index_fh [open "bin/pkg_index.xml" w]
puts $index_fh "<packages>"
foreach pkg $staged_pkgs {
	puts $index_fh "  <pkg name=\"$pkg\"/>"
}
puts $index_fh "</packages>"
close $index_fh

foreach f { greeting.png about.png } {
	set src "${genode_dir}/../pkg/settings_demo/$f"
	if {![file exists $src]} {
		error "settings_demo icon not found: $src"
	}
	exec cp -L $src "bin/$f"
}

#
# Metadata audit BEFORE boot (the configd-absence claim is asserted,
# not assumed): the shipped metadata must declare exactly the Gui and
# File_system sessions, must name the package-private subpath, must
# carry both settings entries, and must contain no configd label.
#
set meta_fh [open "bin/pkg_settings_demo.xml" r]
set meta_txt [read $meta_fh]
close $meta_fh

# strip XML comments first: the shipped comment block names "configd"
# to say there is none, which must not satisfy the absence check
regsub -all {(?s)<!--.*?-->} $meta_txt {} meta_txt

foreach claim {
	{<settings id="greeting"}
	{<settings id="about"}
	{subpath="/app/settings_demo"}
	{name="Gui"}
	{name="File_system"}
} {
	if {[string first $claim $meta_txt] < 0} {
		error "metadata audit FAILED: missing $claim"
	}
}
if {[string first "configd" $meta_txt] >= 0} {
	error "metadata audit FAILED: settings_demo metadata names a configd label"
}
if {[regexp -all {<session name=} $meta_txt] != 2} {
	error "metadata audit FAILED: settings_demo must declare exactly two sessions (Gui, File_system)"
}
puts "metadata audit ok: 2 settings entries, sessions=Gui+File_system, no configd label"

set boot_modules [build_artifacts]
foreach m { pkg_settings_demo.xml pkg_index.xml greeting.png about.png } {
	lappend boot_modules $m
}

build_boot_image $boot_modules

#
# QEMU: seL4 needs >= 1 GiB; no interactive PC drivers and no QMP are
# staged - the probe injects input through nitpicker's Event service
# headlessly (the sponge_de_probe precedent).
#
match_max -d 200000

append qemu_args " -nographic -m 2G "

#
# Gate sequence. run_genode_until gates ONLY the probe markers (the
# probe is the single sequencing actor - its markers are strictly
# ordered in the log, so no gate can race an asynchronous provider or
# pkgd line). The provider/pkgd log evidence is verified below by
# exact counts and causal-order regexps over the accumulated output
# (the supervisor-control precedent: counts, absences and ordering
# cannot be expressed as forward-only log-line gates). Every gate is
# bounded; a probe-side failure exits non-zero with
# 'settings-thirdparty-probe: FAIL' and is rejected by the verdict
# gates below.
#
run_genode_until {.*settings-thirdparty-probe: install settings_demo -> ok.*} 300

global qemu_spawn_id

# The probe sequences every step itself. Wait only for its terminal
# verdict, then validate the accumulated evidence below.
run_genode_until {.*settings-thirdparty-probe: PASS.*} 120 $qemu_spawn_id

#
# Post-gates over the accumulated run output: exact counts, exact
# store XML documents, the causal close-commit order, and the
# configd absence.
#
global output

proc require_count { pattern n what } {
	global output
	set c [regexp -all -- $pattern $output]
	if {$c != $n} {
		puts stderr "FAIL: expected $n x $what, saw $c"
		exit 1
	}
}

proc require_order { pattern what } {
	global output
	if {![regexp -- $pattern $output]} {
		puts stderr "FAIL: causal-order evidence missing: $what"
		exit 1
	}
}

# the two entries opened exactly 3x each (initial, restart-reopen,
# reinstall) and never aliased each other's identity
require_count {settings result settings_demo 'greeting' -> ok started} 3 \
	"greeting '-> ok started' (open, restart-reopen, reinstall)"
require_count {settings result settings_demo 'about' -> ok started} 3 \
	"about '-> ok started' (open, restart-reopen, reinstall)"

# removal: stale opens answer not-installed (14.7), exactly one each
require_count {settings open settings_demo 'greeting' -> not-installed} 1 \
	"greeting stale-open not-installed"
require_count {settings open settings_demo 'about' -> not-installed} 1 \
	"about stale-open not-installed"

# close/restart: close-commit + exit + claim release, one per provider
require_count {persisted greeting} 2 \
	"greeting persists (Enter commit + close commit)"
require_count {persisted note} 2 \
	"about persists (Enter commit + close commit)"
require_count {window closed, exiting} 2 \
	"provider close-to-exit (greeting + about)"
require_count {claim released; next open starts fresh} 2 \
	"claim release on provider close (greeting + about)"

# first start defaults and later restores (retention across restart)
require_count {no store [^\n]+ starting with default} 2 \
	"first-start defaults (greeting + about)"
require_count {restored greeting from store} 2 \
	"greeting restore-from-store (restart + reinstall)"
require_count {restored note from store} 2 \
	"about restore-from-store (restart + reinstall)"
require_count {package-private store enabled at /greeting.xml} 3 \
	"greeting instantiations (open, restart, reinstall)"
require_count {package-private store enabled at /about.xml} 3 \
	"about instantiations (open, restart, reinstall)"

# keystroke validation: exactly one rejection with the exact text
require_count {invalid character \(allowed: A-Z a-z 0-9 space \. , ! \? -\)} 1 \
	"keystroke validation rejection with exact English error"

# exact store documents in the receipt log (the probe validated the
# bytes; these pins make the evidence reviewable from the log alone).
# Readback sequence: S8 G1+A1, S9 G2+A1, S10 G2+A2, S12 G2+A2, S13 G2+A2.
require_count {name=.greeting. value=.Hi there./></settings-demo>} 1 \
	"greeting pre-close store document (value Hi there)"
require_count {name=.note. value=.Note A./></settings-demo>} 2 \
	"about pre-close store document (value Note A)"
require_count {name=.greeting. value=.Hi there!.} 4 \
	"greeting close-committed store document (value Hi there!)"
require_count {name=.note. value=.Note A ok.} 3 \
	"about close-committed store document (value Note A ok)"

# the close chain is causal: dirty type -> close-commit -> exit ->
# claim release -> probe close-done, for BOTH providers
require_order {(?s)typed-dirty greeting.*persisted greeting.*window closed, exiting.*claim released; next open starts fresh.*close-greeting done} \
	"greeting close-commit chain"
require_order {(?s)typed-dirty about.*persisted note.*window closed, exiting.*claim released; next open starts fresh.*close-about done} \
	"about close-commit chain"

# the rejection really was rejected: the '@' typed before the value
# never appears in any store document
if {[regexp {value=[^>]*@} $output]} {
	puts stderr "FAIL: rejected character '@' leaked into a store value"
	exit 1
}

# no shared-DE configuration route or capability in this topology
require_count {configd} 0 \
	"configd mentions in the whole boot (must be absent)"

# the probe verdict
require_count {settings-thirdparty-probe: PASS} 1 \
	"the probe PASS verdict"
require_count {settings-thirdparty-probe: FAIL} 0 \
	"the probe FAIL marker (must be absent)"

if {![regexp {settings-thirdparty-probe: PASS} $output]} {
	puts stderr "FAIL: probe did not reach its PASS verdict"
	exit 1
}

#
# Final explicit PASS marker (verdict, emitted only after every gate
# and count above succeeded).
#
puts "sponge-settings-thirdparty: PASS - two independent entries (greeting/about) discovered and first-painted, keystroke validation rejected the bad char, values persisted as exact per-provider store XML (name=greeting vs name=note, distinct values), retained across provider close/restart with close-commit, remove answered not-installed and reinstall answered started with un-aliased stores, package-private File_system writable, zero shared-DE configd routes or capabilities"
